uction at the taper came Pam had a  conflict with her Broadway place she was  doing that year so then director number  2 les waters came in michael la mirada  was the third third and then and Kaufman  in New York right so it was four what  are your recollections of the shoot I  mean these are scenes that are 10 11 12  pages in some cases I would imagine this  was not a luxuriously long shoot what  are your recollections of having of the  challenges of the day well that was one  of the big challenges but also really  fun I mean an actor wants to have meaty  thing is to do and and feel challenged  and and it was very fast we've been  trying to remember how many actual  shooting days it was but it was probably  in the teens and and so yeah you'd you  know do some very intense emotional  scene one day and then okay what's  tomorrow  oh the 12-page scene that's tomorrow so  it was very challenging but you know  felt like the power on this fun thing  together did you guys talk to each other  and with John since all three of you  play Prime's at some point in the film  about making your performances as primes  similar in any way since I would imagine  it was the same technology and the same  company making these making all three of  the primes did you guys talk to each  other about how those performances would  be or did you just let it happen never  talked about it did you oh I forgot to  that would have prevented you like to  talk about it I think it was that's okay  because we played Prime's at different  stages of their development like mine  was day one and and fresh out of the box  we always fresh out of the box right is  it at all intimidating  to come on to a project when one of the  cast members has done it already were  you feeling like oh my gosh how can we  catch up to Oh  or is that the joy of it to work with  someone who's inhabited the character  for a long time we found it we all found  it fascinating and we would ask  questions like this Louis to do is it 